WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.690 --> 00:00:02.450
- [Instructor] We're
told this is the graph

00:00:02.450 --> 00:00:04.420
of y is equal to 3/2 to the x,

00:00:04.420 --> 00:00:06.060
and that's it right over there.

00:00:06.060 --> 00:00:09.150
Use the graph to find
an approximate solution

00:00:09.150 --> 00:00:12.170
to 3/2 to the x is equal to five.

00:00:12.170 --> 00:00:14.280
So pause this video and
try to do this on your own

00:00:14.280 --> 00:00:16.530
before we work on this together.

00:00:16.530 --> 00:00:19.040
All right, now let's work on this.

00:00:19.040 --> 00:00:21.780
So they already give us a
hint of how to solve it.

00:00:21.780 --> 00:00:24.540
They have the graph of y
is equal to 3/2 to the x,

00:00:24.540 --> 00:00:27.220
they graph it right over here,
and this gives us a hint,

00:00:27.220 --> 00:00:28.800
and especially because they want us

00:00:28.800 --> 00:00:30.670
to find an approximate solution,

00:00:30.670 --> 00:00:32.280
that maybe we can solve this equation,

00:00:32.280 --> 00:00:35.430
or approximate a solution to
this equation through graphing.

00:00:35.430 --> 00:00:37.970
And the way we could do that
is we could take each side

00:00:37.970 --> 00:00:41.340
of this equation and set
them up as a function.

00:00:41.340 --> 00:00:44.080
We could set y equals to each side of it.

00:00:44.080 --> 00:00:46.730
So if we set y equals
to the left hand side,

00:00:46.730 --> 00:00:51.730
we get y is equal to 3/2 to the x power,

00:00:51.930 --> 00:00:54.350
which is what they originally
give us, the graph of that.

00:00:54.350 --> 00:00:58.110
And if we set y equal
to the right hand side,

00:00:58.110 --> 00:01:02.750
we get y is equal to five,
and we can graph that.

00:01:02.750 --> 00:01:06.960
And what's interesting here
is if we can find the x-value

00:01:06.960 --> 00:01:11.370
that gives us the same y-value
on both of these equations,

00:01:11.370 --> 00:01:13.810
well that means that those
graphs are going to intersect.

00:01:13.810 --> 00:01:16.840
And if I'm getting the same
y-value for that x-value

00:01:16.840 --> 00:01:19.018
in both of these, well then that means

00:01:19.018 --> 00:01:24.018
that 3/2 to the x is
going to be equal to five.

00:01:25.020 --> 00:01:27.500
And so we can look at where they intersect

00:01:27.500 --> 00:01:31.560
and get an approximate sense
of what x-value that is.

00:01:31.560 --> 00:01:33.660
And we can see it, at least over here,

00:01:33.660 --> 00:01:38.020
it looks like x is roughly equal to four.

00:01:38.020 --> 00:01:42.800
So x is approximately equal to four.

00:01:42.800 --> 00:01:45.750
And if we wanted to, and
we'd be done at that point,

00:01:45.750 --> 00:01:48.380
if you wanted to, you
could try to test it out.

00:01:48.380 --> 00:01:50.390
You could say hey does
that actually work out.

00:01:50.390 --> 00:01:54.360
3/2 to the fourth power,
is that equal to five.

00:01:54.360 --> 00:01:56.970
Let's see, three to the fourth is 81,

00:01:56.970 --> 00:02:01.970
two to the fourth is 16, it
gets us pretty close to five.

00:02:03.310 --> 00:02:07.110
16 times five is 80, so it's not exact,

00:02:07.110 --> 00:02:08.790
but it gets up pretty close.

00:02:08.790 --> 00:02:10.260
And if you had a graphing calculator

00:02:10.260 --> 00:02:12.930
that could really zoom in
and zoom in and zoom in,

00:02:12.930 --> 00:02:14.880
you would get a value, you would see

00:02:14.880 --> 00:02:19.880
that x is slightly different
than x equals four.

00:02:19.990 --> 00:02:21.010
But let's do another example.

00:02:21.010 --> 00:02:23.330
The key here is that we
can approximate solutions

00:02:23.330 --> 00:02:25.203
to equations through graphing.

00:02:26.390 --> 00:02:31.330
So here we are told this is
the graph of y is equal to,

00:02:31.330 --> 00:02:34.630
so we have this third degree
polynomial right over here,

00:02:34.630 --> 00:02:37.810
use the graph to answer
the following questions.

00:02:37.810 --> 00:02:40.650
How many solutions does the equation

00:02:40.650 --> 00:02:42.820
x to the third minus two x squared minus x

00:02:42.820 --> 00:02:45.770
plus one equals negative one have?

00:02:45.770 --> 00:02:48.650
Pause the video and try
to think about that.

00:02:48.650 --> 00:02:50.110
When we think about solutions to this,

00:02:50.110 --> 00:02:52.960
we can say all right, well
let's imagine two functions.

00:02:52.960 --> 00:02:56.500
One is y is equal to x to
the third minus two x squared

00:02:56.500 --> 00:03:00.120
minus x plus one, which we
already have graphed here.

00:03:00.120 --> 00:03:02.800
And let's say that the other equation,

00:03:02.800 --> 00:03:05.660
or the other function, is
y is equal to negative one.

00:03:05.660 --> 00:03:08.210
And then how many times
do these intersect?

00:03:08.210 --> 00:03:09.920
That would tell us how
many solutions we have.

00:03:09.920 --> 00:03:13.200
So that is y is equal to negative one.

00:03:13.200 --> 00:03:15.580
And so every time they intersect,

00:03:15.580 --> 00:03:18.030
that means we have a solution
to our original equation.

00:03:18.030 --> 00:03:21.770
And they intersect one,
two, and three times.

00:03:21.770 --> 00:03:24.340
So this has three solutions.

00:03:24.340 --> 00:03:26.300
What about the second situation?

00:03:26.300 --> 00:03:28.200
How many solutions does the equation

00:03:28.200 --> 00:03:30.363
all of this business equal two have?

00:03:31.290 --> 00:03:34.580
Well same drill, we could set
y equals to x to the third

00:03:34.580 --> 00:03:36.380
minus two x squared minus x plus one

00:03:36.380 --> 00:03:39.410
and then we could think
about another function,

00:03:39.410 --> 00:03:41.280
what if y is equal to two.

00:03:41.280 --> 00:03:45.640
Well y equals two would be up over there,

00:03:45.640 --> 00:03:47.510
y equals two, and we can see

00:03:47.510 --> 00:03:51.720
it only intersects y equals
all of this business once.

00:03:51.720 --> 00:03:55.380
So this is only going
to have one solution.

00:03:55.380 --> 00:03:57.440
So the key here, and
I'll just write it out,

00:03:57.440 --> 00:03:58.980
and these are screenshots
from the exercise

00:03:58.980 --> 00:04:00.750
on Khan Academy where
you'd have to type in one,

00:04:00.750 --> 00:04:03.440
or in the previous example
you would type in four,

00:04:03.440 --> 00:04:05.350
but these are examples where you can

00:04:05.350 --> 00:04:07.150
take an equation of one variable,

00:04:07.150 --> 00:04:10.740
set both sides of them
independently equal to y,

00:04:10.740 --> 00:04:13.550
graph them, and then think
about where they intersect

00:04:13.550 --> 00:04:15.550
because the x-values where they intersect

00:04:15.550 --> 00:04:18.440
will be solutions to
your original equation.

00:04:18.440 --> 00:04:20.600
And a graph is a useful way

00:04:20.600 --> 00:04:22.530
of approximating what a solution will be,

00:04:22.530 --> 00:04:24.300
especially if you have
a graphing calculator,

00:04:24.300 --> 00:04:26.083
or Desmos, or something like that.

